            {
               "text": "<p style=\"font-style: italic;\" id=\"htChinaZhongguo\"></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Where is the center?\n\nThe image in this section comes from the Kano collection of the Tohoku University Digital Archives (<htcite0>). It shows how Chinese people have pictured the world. On *History Tracers*, this kind of map is always explained by Professor Xiaohuan Zhao, at the start of this text (<htcite1>).\n\nDepending on where you live, China (Zhongguo 中國) is probably not your idea of the \"center of the world\". For you, that center might be the Greenwich Meridian (<htcite2>).\n\nThis idea leads us to a question:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "80851755-2574-4396-9d44-65853e30e59a",
                     "text": "Ricci, Matteo",
                     "page": "Kon'yo bankoku zenzu",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "66858370-a9e8-44a7-88e1-8a2f5a516905",
                     "text": "Xiaohuan Zhao",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9828a108-dfdf-4692-a819-18b690d4a629",
                     "text": "Raikar, S.P.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "22"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: We divide starting from...</b><br /><br />How would you define the center of the planet?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Choosing an answer\n\nIn other texts, we saw that on a number line the center can be zero (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>). But where is the center of the planet? That depends on where you put that zero.\n\nIf zero marks where our species began, then the starting point is Africa. We only learned this thanks to major scientific advances, and the idea is relatively recent (<htcite3>).\n\nEven knowing that humans came from Africa more than 300,000 years ago, different peoples — such as France, Prussia, and the United Kingdom — still chose their own reference points (<htcite4>).\n\nIn <htdate0>, representatives of 25 nations met in Washington, D.C., United States. They voted to make the Greenwich Meridian the prime meridian (the \"zero\" line for maps and time). This line does pass through Africa. Still, they named it after an observatory in London, and France did not join the 22 voters who approved this choice (<htcite5>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9618a83e-f232-49db-84f6-fc3455a3f1d1",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Drawing to count",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "92b5d15c-d26a-4ad9-82af-3262649cf2e4",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking Among the Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b8c8840f-0631-420d-b5b3-7ebbebdb9625",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Integers",
                     "date_time": {
