               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Now we use the same projection and update **Figure 1** to the present day:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img class=\"imgcenter\" src=\"\" id=\"imgChina\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgChina', '0%')\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: Mercator projection from the perspective of China (Zhongguo 中國). © Mapswire (<i><htcite0></i>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/licenses/6\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 IGO</a> license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: Mapping</b><br /><br />We prefer maps without country borders, because borders are human lines. That makes it easier to think clearly.<br /><br />Look at <b>Figures 2 and 3</b> and answer:<br /><br />When we move the continents on the map, do cold regions become warm?<br /><br />Does a desert turn into a forest?<br /><br />Did you ever stop to think that our species is over 300,000 years old (<htcite0>), yet the nations we see today are new?<br /><br />After shifting the continents between maps, is it a problem to put your own region at the center?<br /><br />Did moving the map change anything in your life?<br /><br />If people can define the center any way they like, could some also claim that humanity began where they live — instead of Africa, where it really began?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Humans",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned that the choice of a prime meridian happened recently — and that it is not the only possible one. We also reflected on scenes that show one key idea: people are the same everywhere, no matter where they live.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
